SAS/C interoperability of -msasregparm -mmangle-regparm
=======================================================

Code built with -msasregparm -mmangle-regparm should link and run with
SAS/C __regargs code, in both directions.  These tests check that against
SAS/C 6.58 itself.

gen.py writes, for each group of functions:
  G-callee.c   the functions; each mixes its arguments, in order, into a
               32-bit value, so a swapped or misread argument shows
  G-caller.c   run_G(), which calls each one and stores what it returned
  G-driver.c   main(), which runs run_G() and compares the results with
               the values gen.py computed
The callee and caller halves are C89 that both gcc and SAS/C compile
(sas-abi.h holds the keywords that differ).  The groups:
  noargs  a function without arguments
  core    every sequence of long and pointer arguments up to 6, so every
          way the d0/d1 and a0/a1 slots fill up and spill to the stack
  narrow  char and short arguments and return values, pointer returns
  struct  structures passed and returned by value
  asm     explicit argument registers (SAS/C __asm, gcc __asm ("dN"))
  fpu     doubles, in fp0/fp1 (68020 and 68881)

sas.sh compiles the SAS/C halves into obj/, which is checked in, since
SAS/C does not run in CI:
  ./gen.py && ./sas.sh /path/to/SC
It runs SAS/C under volamos.  The objects use no globals and no multiply
or divide, so they need neither sc.lib nor SAS/C's startup code: main()
is always gcc's.

sas-interop.tcl, sourced by amigaos.exp, links each group's driver with
a caller and a callee half in every pairing and runs it:
  gcc->gcc    -msasregparm -mmangle-regparm on both sides
  gcc->sas    gcc calling SAS/C's @functions
  sas->gcc    SAS/C calling gcc's
  sas->sas    SAS/C on both sides: checks gen.py's values, and our ld
  plain->sas  gcc without either option calling the _functions SAS/C's
              parameters=both adds for stack callers

To run these alone, from the toolchain repository (sas/core for one
group):
  DEJAGNU=$PWD/dejagnu-site.exp make -C build-Linux-m68k-amigaos/gcc \
    check-gcc-c "RUNTESTFLAGS=--target_board=volamos amigaos.exp=sas/*"
On a board without a 68881 (volamos), the fpu group is unsupported;
volamos-fpu runs it.

Known differences
-----------------
- A function without arguments: SAS/C names it @name, -mmangle-regparm
  leaves _name (noargs gcc->sas and sas->gcc fail to link: XFAIL until
  fixed).
- Explicit registers: SAS/C names an __asm function _name whatever its
  registers, -mmangle-regparm _<regs>@name, or @name when the registers
  happen to be d0/d1/a0/a1 (asm gcc->sas and sas->gcc fail to link:
  XFAIL until fixed).
- Structures (XFAIL): SAS/C passes them on the stack whatever their size
  and returns them through a buffer whose address it passes on the
  stack; gcc passes small ones in registers and returns those of up to 8
  bytes in d0/d1.  This differs without -msasregparm too.

Our ld and SAS/C objects
------------------------
Two things keep sas.sh from building the SAS/C halves with its default
options, both linker issues rather than calling convention ones:
- code=near (SAS/C's default) calls with bsr.w.  SAS/C names its code
  section "text" and gcc ".text", and ld puts them in different hunks, so
  a bsr.w from SAS/C code to a gcc function is out of range.  sas.sh
  uses code=far.
- SAS/C refers to initialized data with HUNK_DREL32 relocations in its
  objects, which slink resolves as absolute; ld and vlink treat them as
  a4-relative.  The callers set structure fields one by one, so SAS/C
  uses immediates instead.
